<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paolo Veronese</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paolo Veronese was a painter from Verona who worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, known for his huge, brightly coloured feasts and allegories. His works include The Wedding at Cana and the ceilings of the Doge's Palace. In 1573 he was questioned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> over a Last Supper filled with secular figures and retitled it The Feast in the House of Levi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-veronese.jpg" alt="Paolo Veronese" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Veronese,_avtoportret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Veronese took his usual name from Verona, where he was born in 1528, the fifth child of a stonecutter named Gabriele and his wife Caterina. He was first known as Paolo Spezapreda, after his father's trade, and later took the surname Caliari because his mother was the illegitimate daughter of a nobleman, Antonio Caliari. By 1541 he was apprenticed to Antonio Badile, later his father-in-law, and in 1544 to Giovanni Francesco Caroto, both leading painters in Verona. Trained in the Mannerism then popular in Parma, he soon developed a preference for a more radiant palette.</p><p>In 1551 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giustiniani-family">Giustiniani family</a> commissioned an altarpiece from him for the church of San Francesco della Vigna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and in 1552 Cardinal Ercole Gonzaga commissioned one for Mantua Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Veronese moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1553 after receiving his first state commission, ceiling frescoes in the Hall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> in the Doge's Palace. His ceilings of 1557 in the Marciana Library won a prize judged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> and Jacopo Sansovino.</p><p>In the late 1550s he decorated the Villa Barbaro at Maser, a new building by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>, with frescoes that combine portraits of the family, mythological figures and ceilings opening to blue skies. His Wedding at Cana, painted in 1562–1563 for the monastery of San Giorgio Maggiore, was to cover 66 square metres; the contract required premium pigments and as many figures as possible, and it includes portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tintoretto">Tintoretto</a> and a self-portrait. In 1565 he married Elena Badile, the daughter of his first master. He headed a family workshop that included his brother Benedetto and his sons Carlo and Gabriele, which went on after his death in 1588, signing its works Haeredes Pauli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1573 Veronese completed a Last Supper for the refectory of the Basilica of Santi Giovanni e Paolo, replacing a painting by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> that had been burnt in a fire. At 5.55 by 12.80 metres, it showed a banquet that included German soldiers, dwarves and animals.</p><p>On 18 July 1573 the Venetian Inquisition summoned him to explain these figures, which it considered extraneous to the Last Supper. He told the tribunal that painters take the same liberties as poets and madmen. The Inquisition ordered him to repaint the scene; he opposed this remedy but was compelled to retitle the work The Feast in the House of Levi. The interrogation is described as cautionary rather than punitive. His admirers have included Rubens, Watteau, Tiepolo, Delacroix and Renoir, though his work has been felt not to permit expression of the profound.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paolo Veronese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the great artistic centres of Europe: its school of painting included Giovanni Bellini, Giorgione, Titian, Tintoretto and <b>Paolo Veronese</b>, Andrea Palladio built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from Claudio Monteverdi to Antonio Vivaldi worked in the city, which opened the first public…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Holy Office objected to the “buffoons, drunken Germans, dwarfs” in <b>Paolo Veronese</b>’s huge Last Supper, he was told to change the painting within three months; he changed only its title, to The Feast in the House of Levi, and no more was said.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paolo Veronese"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
